1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,040
You fixed one bug, and in the same file you changed something else.

2
00:00:04,260 --> 00:00:06,840
If you commit both together, your history gets muddy.

3
00:00:07,060 --> 00:00:08,610
Which change was this commit about?

4
00:00:08,830 --> 00:00:13,710
In this video you will stage just one hunk in GitTree, and commit only that part.

5
00:00:14,467 --> 00:00:19,177
A hunk is a block of changed lines, plus a few unchanged lines around it.

6
00:00:19,397 --> 00:00:19,847
This file has two.

7
00:00:20,067 --> 00:00:22,007
The first is a new comment above the class.

8
00:00:22,227 --> 00:00:24,487
The second adds a limit to the query function.

9
00:00:25,267 --> 00:00:30,947
Git keeps your work in three places: the working tree, the staging area, and the repository.

10
00:00:31,167 --> 00:00:32,747
A commit takes only what is staged.

11
00:00:32,967 --> 00:00:35,877
So to commit one hunk, stage that hunk, then commit.

12
00:00:36,097 --> 00:00:37,967
The other hunk stays in your working tree.

13
00:00:38,733 --> 00:00:40,893
Here is GitTree with a small demo project.

14
00:00:41,113 --> 00:00:46,023
The working changes row at the top shows that files have changed. Click it.

15
00:00:46,800 --> 00:00:47,970
Select the file.

16
00:00:48,190 --> 00:00:51,860
GitTree shows its diff, already split into hunks.

17
00:00:52,080 --> 00:00:53,820
Hunk one is the comment.

18
00:00:54,040 --> 00:00:56,010
Hunk two is the query change.

19
00:00:56,767 --> 00:00:59,877
Every hunk has its own Stage Hunk button.

20
00:01:00,097 --> 00:01:04,487
Click it on the first hunk, and it moves to the staging area.

21
00:01:05,267 --> 00:01:07,477
Now the same file appears twice.

22
00:01:07,697 --> 00:01:11,357
Under Unstaged Files, it still holds the hunk we left.

23
00:01:11,577 --> 00:01:14,487
Under Staged Files, it holds the hunk we picked.

24
00:01:15,267 --> 00:01:19,607
Click the staged copy to see exactly what the commit will contain.

25
00:01:19,827 --> 00:01:21,607
Only the comment is there.

26
00:01:22,367 --> 00:01:24,467
Write a commit message, and commit.

27
00:01:24,687 --> 00:01:27,567
This commit holds only the comment change.

28
00:01:28,333 --> 00:01:33,433
The other hunk is still in your working changes, ready for its own commit.

29
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:36,630
Close the diff, and select the new commit.

30
00:01:36,850 --> 00:01:39,430
One file changed, one line added.

31
00:01:39,650 --> 00:01:42,940
That is how every commit stays about one thing.

32
00:01:43,700 --> 00:01:47,120
In plain Git, the same job is git add -p.

33
00:01:47,340 --> 00:01:53,540
Git shows each hunk and asks what to do: y to stage it, n to skip it, s to split it.

34
00:01:53,760 --> 00:01:57,980
Then git diff --staged shows what the commit will contain.

35
00:01:58,200 --> 00:02:01,090
GitTree does the same with one click per hunk.

36
00:02:01,867 --> 00:02:08,237
Two tips. If you change your mind, the same button reads Unstage Hunk on the staged side.

37
00:02:08,457 --> 00:02:12,617
And if one hunk mixes two ideas, pick individual lines instead.

38
00:02:12,837 --> 00:02:14,307
That is the next video.

39
00:02:15,067 --> 00:02:24,257
To recap: a hunk is a block of changed lines, Stage Hunk moves it to the staging area, and a commit takes only what is staged.

40
00:02:24,477 --> 00:02:27,467
Download GitTree free at gittree.app.

41
00:02:27,687 --> 00:02:29,747
See you in the next video.

